Worst Case

The target is the last element or doesn't exist.

[10, 20, 30, 40]
              ↑
Target = 40

We may check all elements.

Space Complexity
O(1)

We only use a few variables.

When to Use Linear Search

Linear Search is useful when:

The array is small.
The array is unsorted.
Simplicity is important.
You only need to search occasionally.
Linear Search vs Binary Search

| Feature | Linear Search | Binary Search |
|---|---|
| Array needs sorting? | No | Yes |
| Best Case | O(1) | O(1) |
| Worst Case | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Easy to implement | Yes | Slightly harder |

Key Takeaways
Linear Search checks elements one by one.
It works on both sorted and unsorted arrays.
It returns the position of the target.
-1 usually means the target was not found.
Worst-case time complexity is O(n).
Space complexity is O(1).
